  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "Japan and Korea customers were charged 100 times their invoice total because the gateway payload converted the amount using a lower-case currency code. `npm test` now passes with 9 tests, including the new regression tests.\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cased the currency (`\"JPY\"` became `\"jpy\"`) for the gateway. It then passed that lower-case code to `toMinor`. The currency registry in `src/lib/currency/registry.js` only knows upper-case codes, so `\"jpy\"` was treated as unknown. Unknown codes fall back to 2 decimal places, so ¥1,500 became 150,000. USD and EUR use 2 decimals anyway, which is why they looked fine.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` now converts the amount using the invoice's stored upper-case currency. Only the `currency` field sent to the gateway is lower-cased.\n- `src/lib/currency/registry.js` now matches currency codes case-insensitively in `exponent`, `isSupported` and `symbol`. A lower-case code can no longer silently fall back to the default exponent.\n\n**Tests added in `test/charge.test.js`:**\n- A JPY invoice of 1500 produces a charge of 1500.\n- A KRW invoice of 20000 produces a charge of 20000.\n- `toMinor` handles lower-case codes, including a 3-decimal case (`kwd`).\n\nOther callers (refunds, payouts, dunning) pass stored upper-case codes, so they were not affected.\n\nI didn't look for charges that already went through at the wrong amount. Customers charged at 100× will need refunds.",
